# File headers and TSDoc

Follow [Google’s technical writing style](https://developers.google.com/tech-writing): active voice, present tense, short sentences, define terms once, lists/tables when they clarify. Restate the change objective in one sentence before editing.

Every new `.ts` file starts with a file header (purpose, not a changelog):

```ts
/**
 * @file CapabilityResolver — apply a tenant override onto a base capability artifact.
 */
```

Document **exported** classes, functions, methods, and public types with TSDoc. Use only [TypeDoc-compatible](https://typedoc.org/documents/Tags.html) tags (`@param`, `@returns`, `@throws`, `@example`, `@file`, `@see`).

- Summary line: imperative mood (“Load the capability by id.”).
- `@param` / `@returns` / `@throws` when the signature is not enough.
- `@example` only for non-obvious call patterns.
- Do not TSDoc trivial private one-liners.
- Do not restate the type in prose (`@param id - a string`).
- Keep comments current. Prefer why over what.

```ts
/**
 * Load the latest capability version for `id`, or a specific version when given.
 *
 * @param id - Catalog capability id (e.g. `loan-payoff`)
 * @param version - Semver capability version; omitted means latest
 * @returns The artifact, or `undefined` if it is not in the catalog
 */
export async function get(
  id: string,
  version?: string,
): Promise<CapabilityArtifact | undefined> {
```
